<!--
  QCar2 navigation behavior tree.

  This is Nav2's stock navigate_to_pose_w_replanning_and_recovery.xml with the
  two recovery behaviors that do not suit an Ackermann-steered car removed:

  * <Spin> asks the robot to rotate on the spot.  A QCar2 steers with its front
    wheels and physically cannot do this, so the behavior just ran its full
    duration commanding a yaw rate the car could never achieve, achieving
    nothing but delay before the RoundRobin advanced to the next recovery.

  <BackUp> is RESTORED (0.25 m at 0.12 m/s).  It was removed earlier because
  the car would reverse indefinitely: the converter latched the last command
  forever, so when BackUp finished and stopped publishing, the reverse command
  stayed applied.  That root cause is fixed — nav2_qcar_command_convert.cpp
  now zeroes speed and steering if /cmd_vel goes quiet for 300 ms — and the
  car is deliberately reversing-capable now, so backing up is exactly the
  right way out of the "wedged against a wall and cannot move" state.

  Recoveries are therefore: back up, then clear both costmaps.  BackUp is
  deliberately FIRST.  The case that actually happens on this car is someone
  stepping in close while it drives: MPPI is then boxed in, its only
  obstacle-free option is to inch forward, and the car crawls (with the
  steering hunting, since the cmd_vel->steering-angle conversion divides by
  speed — now floored, see kSteeringRefSpeed in
  nav2_qcar_command_convert.cpp) until the progress checker calls it stuck.
  Clearing the costmaps first wasted that whole cycle: the person is really
  there, so the LiDAR re-marks them within one update and nothing has
  changed except that another movement_time_allowance has to elapse before
  the next recovery is tried.  Backing up is the move that genuinely creates
  room to plan around them, so it goes first and clearing stays as the
  follow-up for the other case, a stale/ghost mark that no longer exists.
  Note this only ever runs when the car is ALREADY stuck — if MPPI can find
  a way around without stopping, it simply drives it and no recovery is
  reached at all, which is the intended "manoeuvre if you can, back up if
  you cannot" split.  Wait is
  REMOVED per an explicit operator request: the car must always be doing
  something toward the goal, never idling.  Understand the trade this makes:
  Wait existed to give a moving obstacle time to leave before retrying, and
  without it a blockage that clearing + backing up cannot resolve is retried
  immediately in a tight loop instead of pausing.  ObstaclesCritic still stops
  the car short of a real obstacle either way; this only removes the
  deliberate pause between attempts.

  NavigateRecovery's number_of_retries was 6.  nav2_behavior_tree's
  RecoveryNode (recovery_node.hpp) returns FAILURE outright once that count is
  exhausted: bt_navigator then ABORTS the whole goal, every server stops
  publishing, and nav2_qcar_command_convert.cpp's 300 ms watchdog zeroes the
  motors.  The car does not idle at that point, it is DONE: nothing will move
  it again until a fresh goal arrives.  That silent abort, not a hang, is what
  looked like "it stopped for no reason", and 6 cycles at ~1 Hz was only a few
  seconds of genuine trying before giving up.

  The operator's follow-up correction is important: not "try forever", but
  "keep trying continuously (no idle stops), and tell me it genuinely failed
  after about 2 minutes of real effort, or if a path truly cannot be found".
  A raw retry COUNT cannot express a wall-clock deadline: each cycle's real
  duration depends on how long planning, driving and recovery each take, so
  the same number means a different amount of real time every run.  The
  number_of_retries here is left very high (100000, in practice unreachable)
  purely as a defence-in-depth backstop; the actual 2-minute deadline is
  enforced below by wrapping this whole node in a BT.CPP core Timeout
  decorator, which measures real elapsed time regardless of how the cycles
  inside are spent.  The car only stops now via that timeout, cancellation
  (new 2D Pose Estimate, see qcar2_goal_reset.py), or success.

  The Timeout decorator (msec port) is confirmed present in the exact
  libbehaviortree_cpp_v3.so this ROS distribution links (Timeout / TimeoutNode
  symbols found in the library), so it is a BT.CPP core decorator, not a nav2
  plugin: it needs no plugin_lib_names entry and is auto-registered.  It ticks
  its one child normally; if that child has not returned SUCCESS or FAILURE
  within the given milliseconds of when it started running, the decorator
  halts the child and returns FAILURE itself.  bt_navigator resets tree node
  state at the start of each new navigation goal, so this 2 minutes runs per
  goal attempt, not cumulatively across separate goals sent over a session.
  On expiry the wrapped RecoveryNode, meaning everything inside it (planning,
  driving, clearing costmaps, backing up), is cut off together and the goal is
  reported ABORTED, which qcar2_nav_visualizer.py already turns into the
  on-screen "NAV FAILED" banner.  This is therefore also the answer to "tell
  me if a path genuinely
  cannot be made": if ComputePathToPose keeps failing no matter how many
  times the costmaps are cleared, the car keeps trying (per the operator's
  instruction) until the 2 minutes run out, then reports failure rather than
  trying forever in silence.

  REPLANNING IS CONTINUOUS (stock 1 Hz RateController, unconditional
  ComputePathToPose).  For a while this tree held the path and only replanned
  when it became blocked or 8 s had passed ("replan only when needed"), on the
  theory that a fresh plan every second let the path chase the car's drift
  instead of the car being made to steer back.  That was treating a symptom.
  The drift itself had three concrete causes, all now fixed at the source
  (see qcar2_slam_and_nav.yaml and nav2_qcar_command_convert.cpp): the
  turning geometry was sized off a 0.60 rad steering limit the chassis does
  not have, the steering-centre bias restarted from zero every launch, and
  velocity_smoother's angular deadband was discarding every small
  line-holding correction.  With those gone the car can actually track a
  fresh plan, and holding a stale one is purely harmful: when a person steps
  into the corridor the operator saw a new green route drawn but the car
  torn between the old plan the controller still had and the new one, and it
  crept along between the two.  One plan, always current, from the car's
  actual pose, is what a dynamic-obstacle world needs, and is what the
  operator explicitly asked for.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Timeout msec="120000" name="GiveUpAfter2MinutesOfRealEffort">
    <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="100000" name="NavigateRecovery">
      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
        <RateController hz="2.0">
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
            <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Context" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </RateController>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="FollowPath">
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </PipelineSequence>
      <ReactiveFallback name="RecoveryFallback">
        <GoalUpdated/>
        <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
          <BackUp backup_dist="0.30" backup_speed="0.12"/>
          <Sequence name="ClearingActions">
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
          </Sequence>
        </RoundRobin>
      </ReactiveFallback>
    </RecoveryNode>
    </Timeout>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
